00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:01.490 align:start position:0%
 
so the way it works is there are a  number of big observatories around the  world and there are a number of  astronomers around the world and  obviously they all want to use the big  telescopes and there's you know more of  them wanting to use the telescopes and  there is time available so we have to  have some mechanism for deciding who  actually gets to use the telescope's  what science gets done it depends on the  telescope so for something like the  Hubble Space Telescope which was one of  the premier facilities out there then  you're probably talking over  subscription rates of about a factor of  10 so there's people want ten times as  much time on the telescope as there is  available for some of the littler  telescopes it gets down to a factor of  two or three you have to fight off the  other astronomers before you get to use  it we have this standard mechanism that  you get used for lots of different  things whether it's assessing qualities  of grant applications or scientific  papers or whatever called peer review  which essentially means that whatever  you propose to do whatever you write  gets sent to other astronomers who kind  of weigh it up against all the other  applications in using telescopes the way  this works is probably twice a year as  the usual way these things work there's  a call for applications and then there's  a peer review panel basically a  collection of astronomers who come from  the community and the peer review panel  sifts their way through all these  applications grades them all figure it  out which of them get the telescope time  they're supposed to be these honest  brokers in this process these  disinterested parties and you know if  it's if there's an obvious conflict of  interest like you know if it's your own  telescope application obviously you  don't get to assess it so there are ways  for dealing with a very direct conflicts  of interest but really they're just  people that the community puts trust in  if you like it varies a bit from panel  to panel they all tend to work in  different ways some of them you actually  sort of give a grade to the to the  proposals before you even meet and then  you get together and you compare your  grades and kind of refine them from  there others you sort of start from  scratch in the meeting in some of them  everyone assesses all the proposals in  detail in others that's sort of one or  two members of the panel are kind of the  lead for each application you have to  give a score in the end because you have  to decide you know which one's better  than which one so you need some way of  actually ordering things to decide which  ones actually get the telescope time the  problem is just the amount of work  involved and this thing came to a head a  few years ago when I was on the ESO  telescope application panel it's a thing  called the OPC and so this is the this  was the the pile of papers I received  which is just one set of proposals to  look at it's about a hundred proposals  slightly over  under proposals it's this huge mass of  papers and as you can see I've scribbled  on the front of them just because as I'm  going through I'm scoring them trying to  decide you know which ones I like which  ones I don't like even before I get to  the meeting the problem with this is  it's such a big pile of papers that  actually even if you know it takes worth  what it takes me about a week to go  through this lock it's a weak solid work  and then I go off and do the panel and  that's another week solid work so it's  two weeks work just to do this process  and the problem is even if I do a as  conscientious a job as I can I score the  first one by that time I've got to  number 97 I can't remember anything  about number one and so trying to score  number 97 on the same scale that I  scored number one even if I do you know  really a conscientious job you just  can't because you just can't hold that  much stuff in your head at once what got  me to thinking about this whole thing is  actually when a pile like this arrived  on my desk and it was one of that you  know it's the classic displacement  activity in that you know when something  like that comes along you find all sorts  of other things to do and so you know  you go tidy your office and do all those  kinds of things but eventually I run out  of displacement activities and so rather  than actually get on with assessing it I  started trying to think is this some  better way we could be doing this the  scheme I came up with is kind of a way  of distributing the process amongst the  astronomer instead of having a little  panel to do this let's have the whole  community do it the people who apply  should be the people who actually do  that through the assessment and one of  the other issues is because astronomers  do recognize this process is a little  bit of a lottery as to whether or not  you get time there is this temptation to  say I'll put five proposals in because  that way I'm more likely to get the  telescope time and at the moment there's  no penalty really for doing that you  annoy the panel a bit because they see  your name coming up lots of times and  you start irritating them but actually  there isn't really a penalty whereas if  you could come up with a scheme where if  you put a load of applications in you  get a load of work back to do the  assessment the idea I came up with was  this each application that comes in gets  sent out to say five six seven other  people who've applied so each person  will applies will get a pile of say  seven or eight other people's  applications to look at all they have to  do is actually go through that list go  through that pile of applications  they've got so they've only got they've  got 100 they've only got seven or eight  to deal with so it's a manageable thing  you can actually really read that seven  or eight proposals very carefully the  only thing they then have to do  you drank them so this is the best one  this is the worst one they don't give  them scores I don't give them anything  else this is the one I like the most  this is one I like the least and this is  the order in between so everyone's  looked at a different combination of  seven or eight applications because  they've been kind of randomly  distributed and obviously you don't get  your own one back and you have to be a  bit careful you make sure you don't get  anyone from your sony institutions you  so it's a little bit of care taken to  make sure that there's not you know no  chance for dishonesty in this process it  turns out this is a solved problem how  you actually do that it's a thing called  a board account you've got to come  you've got these individual little rank  list and you've got to somehow go to  compile into this global list that  essentially means that you've got to  give every proposal or score that you  can then order to some of the thing with  the highest score ends up at the top and  the thing with the lowest score ends up  at the bottom and this modified board  account is actually very simple in that  all you do is supposing you had eight  proposals each person had eight  proposals to look at all they do is they  rank the list they then give seven  points to the top one six to the next  five four three two one zero so  everything ends up with a score between  0 and n minus 1 we're into the number of  applications you looked at okay each  application has been looked at by 8  different people that means that there  are eight scores for each proposal  somewhere in that ranked list so  somewhere between 0 and 7 you just add  them up so then the sum of those then  gives you the score that each  applications get and obviously if you're  near the top and everyone's a little  rank sub list you'll get something close  to 7 each one you end up with a nice  high score if you're near the bottom  you'll end up with something close to 0  that then gives you a score you can then  globally combine to create this master  list of all the applications and  actually there's a national mobile  connection in this in that the guy  boarder who invented this was an  astronomer and the reason why he  invented it is because he thought that  he was he was trying to get into the  National Academy in France and he  thought that the voting was being rigged  against him so he got very interested in  electoral theory and how you can  actually come up with ways of dealing  with these kinds of things to actually  make sure that you know that final vote  ends up honest and so this this modified  board account that I ended up using to  rank all these things together actually  came from him but obviously applied to  something rather different he really  rank all these proposals together call  me a cynic but one of the first thing  that comes into my head is that if I'm  in this contest and I see one that I  think is outstanding and definitely is  going to beat mine I would have an  interest in panel  that one and putting it at the bottom of  my list so here's the trick and in fact  so this gets into this whole thing  called mechanism design of how you stop  people gaming systems or at least how  you make them game the system in the way  you want them to do which is the  following that once you've created this  global list you then go back to  everyone's little sub lists their own  little seven or eight that they've  assessed and you see whether the global  list where those seven or eight things  appear in the global it's more or less  agree with where they the order that  they put them in not completely because  as I say once in a while people will  disagree in all those kinds of things  but actual thing that you rank the top  kind of appear somewhere near the top of  the global is the thing that you ranked  at the bottom appear somewhere near the  bottom of the global list and so on  because that's an indication that  actually the way you ranked it more or  less agreed with everybody else so  that's fine but if you find somebody  where you know everybody else who looked  at that proposal said it was really good  but you said it was rubbish you start  suspecting more maybe there's some game  that's been going on exactly along these  lines that maybe it was your competitor  and you were trying to do them down so  if your little sub rank list disagrees  with the global list and it looks like  you've been playing their game your  proposal moves down the pile so you have  an incentive to be honest because  actually you're invested in this process  you want your proposal to be in as near  the top of the depart as possible if you  agree more or less with what everybody  else assessed your application moves up  a bit if you disagree with every what  everybody else says about the proposals  your application moves down the list so  you actually have a strong incentive not  to cheat if you're interested in  astronomy and would like to learn more  about deep space and the world's best  telescopes why not check out our sister  channel called deep sky videos we go  inside all sorts of amazing  observatories there and if you've got  more questions about this video don't  worry so did I and you can hear some of  them answered in an extra film we've  already posted over on deep sky videos  so this intro this is the the main  criticism that's been leveled at this as  a way of doing things and I like I'm  happy to concede it as a point

